                en: 'When Czechoslovakia broke up in March 1939, Carpatho-Ukraine declared independence on 15 March under President Avgustyn Voloshyn, but Hungarian troops, with Hitler’s permission, occupied the rest of Ruthenia between 14 and 18 March, crushed resistance and restored the old common border with Poland. Hungary recognised Tiso’s Slovakia, a German protégé, yet on 23 March attacked eastern Slovakia over a border dispute; the “Little War”, which lasted until the 31st, gained Hungary a further strip of 1,697 km² at the eastern end.',
